<p>William Thomas Jackson, Shannon's maternal grandfather, was born in 1895 in Bachelor Town, a mining settlement in Mineral County, Colorado, whose post office was named Teller to distinguish it from other towns of the same name; residents used both names interchangeably. His father, also named William Thomas Jackson, and his mother, Selma Mathilda Carlsson, a Swedish immigrant who had crossed the Atlantic as a nine-year-old girl in 1872, raised a large family in and around Mineral County's mining camps, a sibling generation (Lillie, Alma, Agnes, Gustavus, Elmer Robert, Claude, and others) that the site is still working to document separately and accurately. The family's fortunes took a hard turn in 1917: William Sr.'s own death certificate, located in 2026, confirms he died that October at Woodcroft Hospital, Pueblo, of pneumonia with a contributory cause of "General Paralysis of the Insane" — giving the family's long-held account of commitment to "the insane asylum in Pueblo" a documented medical basis for the first time, though the actual facility was a private hospital rather than the Colorado State Insane Asylum by that name.</p>

<p>William himself was educated at Creede High School and worked in the Mineral County mines from 1910 to 1927, before his 1928 election as Mineral County Treasurer, an office he still held a decade later, by which time he had also become Clerk of the District Court. He registered for the WWI draft in June 1917, and by his own account served with the 1st and 7th Regiments of the U.S. Marine Corps on garrison duty in the Virgin Islands, 1918–1919, service that explains his later treatment at, and burial in, a VA facility and national cemetery. As of the 1920 census he was still single, living independently in Creede and working as a miner. He was active in Creede civic life as a member of the local Elks lodge and the American Legion.</p>

<p>William married Alice Josephine Davis in Creede on 5 July 1921, herself the daughter of Robert Davis and Anna Maud Woodruff, meaning both of Shannon's maternal grandparents trace back to the same small Mineral County mining community. The couple settled in Creede, where they raised four children: Joel, William Charles ("Scoot"), John Richard, and a daughter living today. By 1930, Alice's brother Albert Woodruff "Woody" Davis was living with them. William's own death, in 1957, came at the Veterans' Hospital at Whipple, near Prescott, Arizona, of complications from lung cancer that had spread to his bones. He is buried at Prescott National Cemetery.</p>